<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Parentix — hardened runtime entitlements.

  Short, and short on purpose. The agent asks macOS for nothing beyond what an
  ordinary application gets:

  - It is **not sandboxed**. `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is absent, which is
    what lets the agent spawn `lsappinfo`, `ioreg` and `networksetup` and send a
    signal to another process. A sandboxed build could do none of those, and
    there is no entitlement that would give them back — the sandbox is the wrong
    container for a device agent, which is why this ships outside the Mac App
    Store.
  - There is **no `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`**, and that
    is a decision rather than an omission. A content-filter or DNS-proxy Network
    Extension is Apple's blessed way to do website filtering, and it is gated
    behind an entitlement granted by application, on a review cycle, with the
    extension itself needing user approval in System Settings. The launchd helper
    reaches the same place with `networksetup` and no Apple negotiation. If the
    entitlement is ever granted, `NEDNSProxyProvider` is the better home for
    `src/dns/proxy.js` and the rest of the agent would not change.

  `allow-jit` and `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` are Electron's, not ours:
  V8 needs both under the hardened runtime, and notarisation fails without them.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
